[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The canon is read in bounded chunks: partial views cut by root, closure, system or query for a model's context

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PARTIAL-VIEWS-FOR-A-BOUNDED-CONTEXT
TITLE: The canon is read in bounded chunks: partial views cut by root, closure, system or query for a model's context
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The canon is not meant to sit in any agent's context whole. A model reads a
bounded chunk of it -- one node with its neighbours, one closure, one root,
one system, one query's table -- and the rest stays on disk. Today only one
bounded chunk exists: the one-node packet render.py --uid produces. Everything
else is unbounded: the whole-canon payload is over a megabyte, the ready
packet renders an entire closure (the sheet measured one at 57% of the
corpus), and a view's page is meant for a person, not a context window.

The mechanism to build: a loader that cuts the canon into chunks by the same
selectors the view already understands -- a root, a closure, a system, a
query word -- at a size a model can hold, with the fingerprint state and the
backlinks of each chunk, so partial views serve an agent as the page serves a
person. Which selectors, what a chunk carries, and how an agent asks for the
next one are the design.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Raised by the operator on 2026-08-30 while bootstrapping a second agent onto the canon: the whole is not meant to be in every agent's full context, and nothing today loads a part of it differently for a partial view.
<<<
